course: dictionary
lesson: 2607
title: Depressed: one word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. To know which one a writer means, read the words around it. These words are its context, and they leave clues. «Going through a rough patch» can mean being depressed or in a mental health crisis, or it can simply mean having a hard time. Only the surrounding words show which one is meant.
explain: In everyday talk, people say «I'm so depressed» about small things, like a wet picnic. Real depression is an illness, and it is serious. If you feel very sad or hopeless for a long time, tell a trusted adult, such as a parent, teacher or school nurse.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. If you read «The town was depressed, with closed factories», the clue is closed factories and few jobs, so depressed means poor, not sad. The clues tell you which meaning fits.
fact: Depressed is an adjective, and its past form comes from the verb depress.
fact: «Depressed» means very sad and without hope, often for a long time.
fact: In health, depression is an illness that needs help from a doctor or counsellor.
fact: «A depressed town» means a poor place with few jobs and little business.
fact: «Depressed» can also mean pushed down, as in «a depressed button», or lower than the land around it.
exercise: «After weeks of feeling empty and hopeless, Sam felt depressed and told his mum.» What does depressed mean here? | Lower than the land around it | Full of energy | Pushed down, like a button | *Very sad and without hope for a long time
exercise: «The old mill town was depressed, with closed factories and few jobs.» What does depressed mean in this sentence? | Lower than a hill | Very sad about the weather | Pushed down into the ground | *Poor, with few jobs and little business
exercise: «Ella pressed the button and it stayed depressed.» What does depressed mean? | *Pushed down | Very sad | Glowing brightly | Poor, with few jobs
test: «Going through a rough patch» can mean real depression or just a hard time. Which clue best shows the writer means real depression? | It says the person was in a bad mood for one afternoon | It says it was raining all day | *The person has felt hopeless for weeks and needs help from a doctor | It says the story was funny
test: Ruby says «I'm so depressed» because her bus is late. What is the best answer? | She is a town with few jobs | She is pushed down into her seat | She has a serious illness | *She is annoyed, but real depression is an illness and is much more serious
exam: «The depressed area of ground lies lower than the land around it.» What does depressed mean in this sentence? | Very sad | Pushed down, like a button | *Lower than the area around it | Poor, with few jobs
